  "OverallSummary": "The case involves Kostas Tsamos, a former General Securities Representative with Spartan Capital Securities, LLC, who engaged in excessive trading in six customers' accounts between February 2016 and April 2022. Four of the affected customers were seniors. FINRA found that Tsamos willfully violated Regulation BI's Best Interest Obligation (specifically the Care Obligation) and FINRA Rules 2111 and 2010 by recommending excessive trades that were unsuitable and not in the customers' best interests.\n\nTo determine excessive trading, FINRA applied standard quantitative metrics: (1) turnover rates and (2) cost-to-equity ratios. FINRA noted that turnover rates of six or more or cost-to-equity ratios above 20% generally indicate excessive trading. Tsamos's trading in the six accounts resulted in extremely high annualized turnover rates of 10 to 21 and annualized cost-to-equity ratios of 38% to 82%—all well above these thresholds.\n\nThe excessive trading generated $958,948 in total trading costs, including $849,576 in commissions for Tsamos, while causing over $1.03 million in total realized losses for the customers. For the three customers specifically detailed in the case: Customer A, a 78-year-old retiree, experienced an 18 turnover rate, 52% cost-to-equity ratio, $182,860 in trading costs, and $123,622 in losses; Customer B, a 65-year-old real estate professional, experienced an 11 turnover rate, 38% cost-to-equity ratio, $214,311 in trading costs, and $253,388 in losses; and Customer C, a 58-year-old contractor, experienced a 21 turnover rate, 82% cost-to-equity ratio, $257,610 in trading costs, and $343,960 in losses.\n\nFINRA suspended Tsamos for 17 months from associating with any FINRA member in all capacities. No monetary sanctions were imposed due to Tsamos's demonstrated inability to pay."
